How the kinds of component are governed
---------------------------------------

  COMPILERS / TOOLCHAINS produce the user's ROM. Several are GPL or LGPL, but
  their licences do not reach the compiled output: gcc's runtime library
  carries the GCC Runtime Library Exception, SDCC's runtime libraries carry a
  linking exception, and the other assemblers and linkers place no
  conditions on what they produce. A game compiled with GPL gcc, cc65 or
  sdcc is the author's own work, freely licensable. The GPL obligation falls
  on romdev as the redistributor of the compiler binaries. It is met by
  publishing the build recipes, romdev's patches and the pinned upstream
  sources in the source repository (https://github.com/monteslu/romdev):
  see packages/romdevtools/scripts/BUILD_MAP.md (which recipe builds which
  package) and packages/romdevtools/scripts/versions.json (every upstream
  URL and pinned commit). The recipes and patches are not in the npm
  tarballs.

  EMULATOR CORES are a development instrument: running a game on a core puts
  none of the core's code into the game. A core's licence governs the
  redistribution of the core binary. Most are GPL. Two are non-commercial
  (below), which is why a romdev distribution that bundles them is free for
  non-commercial use rather than OSI open source.

  THE STATIC RECOMPILER is the exception: it copies code ported from the
  reference cores into its output. Every recompiled cart carries the licence
  of the core it was ported from (see "Recompiled carts").


