Firmware and BIOS
-----------------
romdev ships no console manufacturer's BIOS or firmware. Where a system
needs one, romdev uses a free replacement or its own code, or the user
supplies the image.

  C64         MEGA65 Open ROMs (https://github.com/MEGA65/open-roms),
              GPL-3.0 / LGPL-3.0, a clean-room KERNAL, BASIC and CHARGEN,
              in romdev-core-vice roms/ with their licence files. Upstream
              vice-libretro compiles Commodore's ROMs into the core; romdev
              builds VICE with that disabled and redistributes none of them.
              Set ROMDEV_C64_ROM_DIR to use original ROMs you own instead.
  MSX         C-BIOS (https://cbios.sourceforge.net), BSD-style licence, in
              romdev-core-bluemsx bios/ (licence in each cbios.txt).
  PlayStation OpenBIOS from PCSX-Redux (https://github.com/grumpycoders/
              pcsx-redux), MIT, embedded in romdev-core-beetle-psx-hw.
  Dreamcast   Flycast's reios HLE BIOS (part of Flycast, GPL-2.0).
  GBA         mGBA's own HLE BIOS (part of mGBA, MPL-2.0).
  N64         build({platform:'n64'}) adds romdev's own minimal IPL3 boot
              stub (MIT) to the ROM it produces.
  SNES        The SPC700 boot program in recompiled SNES carts and in
              romdev's SNES path is romdev's own code (MIT), written from
              the documented behaviour of the console's boot ROM.
              Coprocessor firmware is supplied by the user and never ships
              with romdev: the ST010/ST011 and ST018 programs (for
              recompiled carts through options.firmware; for the snes9x and
              bsnes cores as st011.rom / st018.rom in the system directory),
              and likewise the DSP-1 data ROM, which a DSP-1 cart needs
              supplied as firmware.
  PICO-8      FAKE-08 needs no BIOS.


