Recompiled carts (WAT and native backends)
------------------------------------------
  disasm({target:'recompile', emit:'wasmcart'}) turns a ROM into a wasmcart
  (backends: src/analysis/recompile/wat/<platform>/ in this package), and
  emit:'sync32' turns a Genesis ROM into a sync32 .s32
  (src/analysis/recompile/native/genesis/). A recompiled cart does not run on
  an emulator: so that it matches its reference core frame for frame, the
  backend builds the core's machine model INTO the cart, from code ported by
  hand from the core's source or generated mechanically from it. Every cart
  a backend builds contains that code, so the cart is under that code's
  licence.

  romdev's own parts of those directories (the CPU instruction lowerers, the
  dispatch and layout code, the WAT assembler, the packer) are MIT, and each
  backend directory's NOTICE.txt lists which files are ported. In a built
  cart the two are combined, so the cart as a whole follows this table.
  Commits are short here; the full ones are in every cart's licence entry
  (and in versions.json in the source repository).

  NES (wat/nes)
    from     fceumm: PPU, APU, CPU timing, mappers, frame loop
             https://github.com/libretro/libretro-fceumm @ 3a84a6fd
    licence  GPL-2.0-or-later
    means    sharing the cart requires offering its complete corresponding
             source under the GPL.

  Game Boy / Game Boy Color (wat/gb)
    from     gambatte: PPU, APU, MBCs, timers, CPU timing; blipper resampler
             https://github.com/libretro/gambatte-libretro @ 3262c2aa
    licence  GPL-2.0-only AND MIT (blipper)
    means    sharing the cart requires offering its complete corresponding
