Bundled data  (cheat database, used by cheats({op:'lookup'|'apply'|'make'}))
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The cheat database ships in the romdev_game_codes package (index/), a
  pre-parsed, per-platform index built from the community-aggregated
  RetroArch / libretro-database cheat collection (the same data RetroArch,
  RetroDECK and Batocera ship). These codes are an aggregation assembled over
  years from Game Genie / Pro Action Replay code books and community cheat
  dumps; the collection has no single uniform license and individual codes
  carry no stated provenance. romdev redistributes a pre-parsed form (game
  name to decoded address/value entries) rather than the raw .cht files, and
  uses it only as a read/inspect aid and for non-destructive cheat
  application. It is not linked into any built game and does not affect the
  license of games you make. Source:
  https://github.com/libretro/libretro-database (cht/). The index is rebuilt
  from a local .cht tree with packages/romdevtools/scripts/build-cheat-index.mjs
  in the source repository.

SNES boot ROM and chip data - romdev's own, or the user's
--------------------------------------------------------
  The SPC700 sound CPU starts from a 64-byte boot ROM (the IPL) that runs the
  upload protocol over ports $2140-$2143. Upstream snes9x and bsnes embed
  Nintendo's. romdev uses its own instead: a clean-room reimplementation
  written from the public description of the protocol and of the state it
  hands over (romdevtools scripts/spc700-ipl/ipl.asm, MIT), assembled with
  asar. Its port timing was matched by measuring the ports from outside
  only, so a program uploads on the same SPC700 cycles as on the console.
  The snes9x core, the bsnes core and every recompiled SNES cart run it; the
  builds replace the upstream array (scripts/spc700-ipl/install-ipl.mjs).

  romdev ships no chip firmware, chip data ROM or boot ROM. snes9x embeds the
  DSP-1 data ROM (and a sine table taken from it), the DSP-3 data ROM and the
  ST010's data ROM tables; bsnes embeds the DSP-1 data ROM, the ST010 tables,
  the Cx4 data ROM and the Super Game Boy boot ROMs. romdev builds both with
  those tables emptied (scripts/recompile-gen/snes9x-chips/hle-data.mjs,
  scripts/patches/bsnes/strip-firmware.mjs) and takes the user's files
  instead: snes9x reads dsp1b.data.rom, dsp3.data.rom or st010.data.rom from
  its system directory, a recompiled cart is built with the same file
  (options.firmware), and bsnes reads each chip's firmware (and sgb_boot.bin /
  sgb2_boot.bin) from its system directory. Without the file such a cart is
  refused with the file and its size named; bsnes runs a Cx4 cart with its
  high-level emulation when cx4.data.rom is absent.


